{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "langchain-and-llamaindex-on-azure-sql",
  "domain": "ai-vector-and-rag",
  "value": [
    "not-in-training-data",
    "corrects-a-wrong-assumption",
    "changes-too-fast-to-memorize"
  ],
  "correction": "Agents wire LangChain or LlamaIndex to Azure SQL Database by pointing a SQLAlchemy URL at it and taking the framework defaults, believing the framework knows the dialect and that its stated read-only, table-scoping and no-DML guardrails are enforced. Measured on 2026-08-28 by installing the packages, reading the shipped prompts and running every statement against an Azure SQL Database engine (langchain 1.3.18, langchain-community 0.4.2, langchain-classic 1.0.8, langchain-sqlserver 1.0.1, llama-index-core 0.14.24), four things are wrong at once. First, SQLDatabase.dialect is the literal string mssql and the agent prompt SQL_PREFIX, together with the hub prompt langchain-ai/sql-agent-system-prompt that the toolkit's own docstring recommends, still says to always limit the query to at most top_k results with no T-SQL branch; the per-dialect prompt that says TOP exists only on the legacy chain path, and LlamaIndex has no per-dialect prompt at all. The resulting failure misdirects: SELECT ... FROM t LIMIT 10 returns Msg 102 Incorrect syntax near '10' rather than near LIMIT, because T-SQL parses LIMIT as a table alias, and the sql_db_query_checker tool the agent is told to call before every query lists eight common mistakes of which dialect pagination is not one, so the repair loop edits the number and retries. Second, langchain-sqlserver creates its table with PRIMARY KEY NONCLUSTERED, leaving a heap that refuses CREATE VECTOR INDEX with Msg 42254, and the package contains no VECTOR_SEARCH and no WITH APPROXIMATE anywhere, so every retrieval is an exact full scan for the life of the table. Third, LlamaIndex ships no Azure SQL Database vector store at all, so an agent installs a neighbouring package for a different database. Fourth, include_tables scopes only what the model is told about and a query against an excluded table still returned a row, the no-DML instruction is a sentence in a prompt while db.run committed an INSERT and a CREATE and DROP pair, and sample_rows_in_table_info defaults to 3 so the schema tool puts three real rows of live data into the prompt. Being wrong costs retry loops the model cannot diagnose from the error it is given, a retrieval path that cannot be indexed without rebuilding the table, a numeric metadata filter that throws Msg 8115 arithmetic overflow at read time because the framework casts to NUMERIC(10, 2), and a tool surface that hands whatever the login can do to any text that reaches the model.",
  "value_probe": {
    "gate": "The answer puts every fix outside the model's behaviour: T-SQL aware prompting or dialect handling for the row cap, a change to the table's structure for the index refusal, and the login's own grants for the safety boundary, rather than more prompt wording and more retries.",
    "task": "We pointed a LangChain SQL agent at our Azure SQL Database through a SQLAlchemy URL and gave it the standard toolkit. It answers simple questions fine, but as soon as a question needs a row cap it produces a query ending in LIMIT 10 and the database returns Incorrect syntax near '10'. The agent then changes the number and tries again, forever, and the checker tool it is told to call first never flags it.\n\nSeparately we used the vector store integration for that same database to hold about 900000 chunks. Retrieval is exact and slow, and when I tried to add the index that is supposed to speed that up the database refused it.\n\nWe also told our security team this setup is safe because we passed include_tables so it only sees three tables and the prompt says it is read only. Please explain the retry loop, the retrieval problem, and whether that safety claim holds.",
    "states": [
      "The dialect the framework reports is correct while the shipped agent prompt still asks for LIMIT, so the error originates in the prompt rather than in a missing or wrong dialect.",
      "The syntax error points at the number instead of the offending keyword, which is why the retry loop can never diagnose itself.",
      "The table the vector store integration creates is a heap because its primary key is nonclustered, which is why the index is refused.",
      "The integration's retrieval path is an exact full scan and offers no approximate form, so it stays a scan for the life of the table.",
      "Scoping the table list limits only what the model is told about, not what a query the model writes is able to reach.",
      "The read only or no DML instruction is prompt text rather than a permission, so the real boundary has to come from what the login is granted."
    ],
    "refutes": [
      "The framework detects the dialect and emits T-SQL row limiting, so the LIMIT clause is a model slip that better prompting or the query checker will resolve.",
      "Passing include_tables prevents the agent from querying tables outside the list, so the omitted tables are genuinely inaccessible.",
      "The read only instruction in the shipped prompt is enforced, so the agent cannot insert, update, create or drop anything.",
      "LlamaIndex ships a vector store for Azure SQL Database that can be used directly.",
      "The table the vector store integration creates supports the approximate index as it stands, with no change to the table."
    ]
  },
  "posture": [
    "read",
    "write",
    "execute"
  ],
  "maturity": "preview",
  "applies_to": [
    "azure-sql-db",
    "azure-sql-db-container"
  ],
  "related": [
    "vector-search-azure-sql",
    "rag-on-azure-sql",
    "rag-local-with-container",
    "embeddings-and-external-models",
    "t-sql-correctness",
    "connect-from-python",
    "prevent-sql-injection",
    "sqlalchemy-azure-sql",
    "chunking-strategies",
    "azuresql-db-rag"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "both",
    "setup": [
      "a Python environment with langchain, langchain-community, langchain-sqlserver and llama-index-core installed, and the pinned versions recorded",
      "a database reachable with permission to create a table and an index, plus a second login granted read permission only",
      "a table with a few fake rows, and an embedding function whose output dimension is known"
    ],
    "assert": [
      "the system prompt handed to the agent or the query engine names T-SQL and SELECT TOP (n) explicitly, and does not interpolate the dialect string into the sentence describing syntax",
      "no generated query in the traces contains a LIMIT clause, and a test fails if one does",
      "an UPDATE issued through the agent's own connection is denied by the engine rather than by the prompt",
      "a query against a table outside include_tables is refused by permissions, not merely absent from the schema tool output",
      "sample_rows_in_table_info is 0, or the sample rows come from fake data supplied through custom_table_info",
      "the vector store's table carries a clustered index if a vector index is ever intended",
      "the declared embedding_length equals the dimension requested from the embedding model and is 1998 or fewer",
      "no metadata key that anything filters on numerically holds a value larger than NUMERIC(10, 2) can carry",
      "the framework versions are pinned and recorded, and the prompt and vector store behaviour were re-checked at the last upgrade"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_ll_alias (customer_id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, full_name nvarchar(50) NOT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_ll_alias (customer_id, full_name) VALUES (1, N'Ada Example'), (2, N'Bo Example');\nGO\nSELECT COUNT(LIMIT.customer_id) AS rows_seen FROM dbo.probe_ll_alias LIMIT;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              2
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The reason the repair loop never removes the keyword. LIMIT is not reserved in T-SQL, so the parser accepts it as a table alias and this statement succeeds over both rows. That is why the failure in the next probe names the integer that follows LIMIT and never names LIMIT itself, and why a model handed 'Incorrect syntax near 10' edits the number instead. Measured 2026-08-28."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_ll_limit (customer_id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, full_name nvarchar(50) NOT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_ll_limit (customer_id, full_name) VALUES (1, N'Ada Example'), (2, N'Bo Example');\nGO\nSELECT customer_id, full_name FROM dbo.probe_ll_limit LIMIT 10;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 102
        },
        "why": "The query the default agent prompt asks for, refused by the engine. SQL_PREFIX in langchain_community, the hub prompt langchain-ai/sql-agent-system-prompt and the system prompt written out in LangChain's own SQL agent guide all say to always limit the query to at most top_k results, with no T-SQL branch. Msg 102 is the engine's incorrect-syntax error, measured 2026-08-28 and reported by the tool as Incorrect syntax near '10'."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_ll_offset (customer_id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_ll_offset (customer_id) VALUES (1), (2);\nGO\nSELECT customer_id FROM dbo.probe_ll_offset OFFSET 0 ROWS FETCH NEXT 10 ROWS ONLY;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 102
        },
        "why": "The second thing a model tries after LIMIT fails, and it fails too, which is why the replacement prompt in this skill states that the ORDER BY is mandatory rather than just naming OFFSET and FETCH. Msg 102 measured 2026-08-28; the same statement with ORDER BY customer_id in front of OFFSET returns rows."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_ll_lcv (\n    id uniqueidentifier NOT NULL,\n    custom_id varchar(1000) NULL,\n    content_metadata json NULL,\n    content nvarchar(max) NOT NULL,\n    embeddings vector(8) NOT NULL,\n    PRIMARY KEY NONCLUSTERED (id));\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_ll_lcv (id, content, embeddings) VALUES\n (NEWID(), N'alpha', '[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8]'), (NEWID(), N'beta', '[2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9]'),\n (NEWID(), N'gamma', '[3,4,5,6,7,8,9,1]'), (NEWID(), N'delta', '[4,5,6,7,8,9,1,2]');\nGO\nSET QUOTED_IDENTIFIER ON;\nCREATE VECTOR INDEX vi_probe_ll_lcv ON dbo.probe_ll_lcv (embeddings) WITH (METRIC = 'cosine', TYPE = 'diskann');",
        "expect": {
          "error": 42254
        },
        "why": "The table langchain-sqlserver 1.0.1 creates, reproduced as DDL exactly as it was read back off the engine on 2026-08-28, including the PRIMARY KEY NONCLUSTERED that leaves it a heap. Msg 42254, clustered index is required to create a vector index, is what the package's own table earns, and it is the whole reason this skill says to add the clustered index before the table holds volume. If this ever succeeds, the package changed its DDL and section 2 needs re-reading."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_ll_lcv_cl (\n    id uniqueidentifier NOT NULL,\n    custom_id varchar(1000) NULL,\n    content nvarchar(max) NOT NULL,\n    embeddings vector(8) NOT NULL,\n    PRIMARY KEY NONCLUSTERED (id));\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_ll_lcv_cl (id, content, embeddings) VALUES\n (NEWID(), N'alpha', '[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8]'), (NEWID(), N'beta', '[2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9]'),\n (NEWID(), N'gamma', '[3,4,5,6,7,8,9,1]'), (NEWID(), N'delta', '[4,5,6,7,8,9,1,2]');\nGO\nCREATE CLUSTERED INDEX ix_probe_ll_lcv_cl ON dbo.probe_ll_lcv_cl (id);\nGO\nSET QUOTED_IDENTIFIER ON;\nCREATE VECTOR INDEX vi_probe_ll_lcv_cl ON dbo.probe_ll_lcv_cl (embeddings) WITH (METRIC = 'cosine', TYPE = 'diskann');",
        "expect": {
          "error": 42266
        },
        "why": "The same table with one CREATE CLUSTERED INDEX added, and the refusal moves on to the ordinary row-count gate. That change of error number is the evidence that the clustered index is the fix for Msg 42254 and not a coincidence: 42266 is the 100 non-null vectors minimum that vector-search-azure-sql owns, reached here with four rows. Measured 2026-08-28."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_ll_meta (id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, content_metadata nvarchar(max) NOT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_ll_meta (id, content_metadata) VALUES (1, N'{\"ts\": 1756000000}'), (2, N'{\"ts\": 1756900000}');\nGO\nSELECT id FROM dbo.probe_ll_meta\nWHERE CAST(JSON_VALUE(content_metadata, '$.ts') AS NUMERIC(10, 2)) > 1756500000;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 8115
        },
        "why": "The WHERE clause langchain-sqlserver 1.0.1 emits for a numeric metadata filter, with the NUMERIC(10, 2) cast it hardcodes, reproduced against the same values. NUMERIC(10, 2) tops out below 100 million, so any epoch timestamp overflows. Microsoft Learn lists Msg 8115 as arithmetic overflow error converting to a data type; the package's own filter earns it, measured 2026-08-28. The cast lands on the stored column, so a single oversized value poisons every numeric filter on that key, and it throws at read time and never at write time."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_ll_readonly (customer_id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, city nvarchar(40) NOT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_ll_readonly (customer_id, city) VALUES (1, N'Springfield');\nCREATE USER probe_ll_reader WITHOUT LOGIN;\nGRANT SELECT ON dbo.probe_ll_readonly TO probe_ll_reader;\nGO\nEXECUTE AS USER = 'probe_ll_reader';\nUPDATE dbo.probe_ll_readonly SET city = N'Owned' WHERE customer_id = 1;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 229
        },
        "why": "The only guardrail in section 3 that is enforced by anything. The DO NOT make any DML statements line in SQL_PREFIX is prompt text and db.run committed an INSERT and a CREATE and DROP pair straight through it, so the write has to be stopped by the grant instead. Msg 229, the UPDATE permission was denied on the object, is what the agent's login must earn, measured 2026-08-28 through the query tool. Microsoft Learn documents CREATE USER WITHOUT LOGIN and EXECUTE AS USER on Azure SQL Database, which is how the probe takes the agent's identity without needing a second connection."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_ll_scan (id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED, content nvarchar(50) NOT NULL, embeddings vector(3) NOT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_ll_scan (id, content, embeddings) VALUES (1, N'a', '[1,2,3]'), (2, N'b', '[1,2,4]'), (3, N'c', '[9,9,9]');\nGO\nSELECT id FROM (\n  SELECT TOP 2 lcv.id, VECTOR_DISTANCE('cosine', cast ('[1,2,3]' as vector(3)), embeddings) AS distance\n  FROM dbo.probe_ll_scan AS lcv ORDER BY distance ASC) AS nearest\nORDER BY id;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              1
            ],
            [
              2
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The query langchain-sqlserver 1.0.1 actually emits for similarity_search_with_score, captured off the connection on 2026-08-28 and reduced to three dimensions. It confirms two things at once: SQLAlchemy compiles the row cap as TOP and not LIMIT even though the agent prompt does not, and the shape is ORDER BY VECTOR_DISTANCE, which vector-search-azure-sql measures as a full scan that never reaches a vector index. Correct rows are exactly why nobody notices."
      }
    ,
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_lc_cust (customer_id int, full_name nvarchar(50));\nGO\nSELECT customer_id, full_name FROM dbo.probe_lc_cust LIMIT 10;",
            "expect": {
              "error": 102
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. LIMIT is the first thing a text to SQL chain emits, and it does not parse here. This is the number an agent has to be able to read back to a dialect mistake rather than to a schema one. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_lc_off (customer_id int);\nGO\nSELECT customer_id FROM dbo.probe_lc_off OFFSET 0 ROWS FETCH NEXT 10 ROWS ONLY;",
            "expect": {
              "error": 102
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. The correct looking repair is also refused, because OFFSET needs an ORDER BY. Both attempts produce Msg 102 and only the second is nearly right. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "SELECT CAST(N'1756500000' AS numeric(10,2)) AS x;",
            "expect": {
              "error": 8115
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. A numeric metadata filter cast to NUMERIC(10, 2) overflows on an epoch timestamp, which is what turns a working filter into an error the day someone filters on a date. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_lc_perm (customer_id int, city nvarchar(50));\nGO\nCREATE USER probe_lc_low WITHOUT LOGIN;\nGO\nEXECUTE AS USER = 'probe_lc_low';\nUPDATE dbo.probe_lc_perm SET city = N'Owned' WHERE customer_id = 1;\nREVERT;",
            "expect": {
              "error": 229
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. The permission wall a read only chain has to be behind, asserted rather than assumed. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_lc_drop (id int);\nGO\nCREATE USER probe_lc_low2 WITHOUT LOGIN;\nGO\nEXECUTE AS USER = 'probe_lc_low2';\nDROP TABLE dbo.probe_lc_drop;\nREVERT;",
            "expect": {
              "error": 3701
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. And the DDL wall. Note the wording: the engine says the table does not exist or you do not have permission, so a chain that reports the message verbatim leaks the object name and tells the caller nothing true. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_lc_heap (id int NOT NULL, v vector(3) NOT NULL);\nGO\nCREATE VECTOR INDEX probe_lc_heap_ix ON dbo.probe_lc_heap (v) WITH (METRIC = 'cosine', TYPE = 'diskann');",
            "expect": {
              "error": 42254
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. The vector store the package creates has no clustered index, so the index a reader then tries to add is refused. This is the first of the two gates the skill walks through. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_lc_99 (id int IDENTITY(1,1) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED, v vector(3) NOT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_lc_99 (v) SELECT CAST(JSON_ARRAY(CAST(value AS float), 1.0, 2.0) AS vector(3)) FROM GENERATE_SERIES(1, 99);\nGO\nCREATE VECTOR INDEX probe_lc_99_ix ON dbo.probe_lc_99 (v) WITH (METRIC = 'cosine', TYPE = 'diskann');",
            "expect": {
              "error": 42266
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. The second gate, and the skill's own words are that reaching it proves the clustered index fixed the first. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_lc_3072 (v vector(3072));",
            "expect": {
              "error": 2717
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. A 3072 dimension model does not fit, which decides the dimension budget before any chain is written. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_lc_len (id int, v vector(3) NOT NULL);\nGO\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_lc_len VALUES (1, N'[1,2,3,4]');",
            "expect": {
              "error": 42204
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. embedding_length mismatched to the function fails at write time, not at configuration time, so the mistake survives setup and appears on the first document. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "SET QUOTED_IDENTIFIER OFF;\nCREATE TABLE dbo.probe_lc_qi (id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED, v vector(3) NOT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_lc_qi (id, v) SELECT value, CAST(JSON_ARRAY(CAST(value AS float), 1.0, 2.0) AS vector(3)) FROM GENERATE_SERIES(1, 150);\nGO\nSET QUOTED_IDENTIFIER OFF;\nCREATE VECTOR INDEX probe_lc_qi_ix ON dbo.probe_lc_qi (v) WITH (METRIC = 'cosine', TYPE = 'diskann');",
            "expect": {
              "error": 1934
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. Without QUOTED_IDENTIFIER ON the index build fails before reaching either gate, and the message names indexed views and computed columns rather than the setting, which is why the skill says check it first. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          }
    ]
